															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/thegreatgimmick" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2890370); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2890370&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606467195" alt="TheGreatGimmick" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2890370)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/thegreatgimmick" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2890370); return false;" >TheGreatGimmick</a></span> <span class="odate time_1480816689 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">04 Dec 2016 01:58</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2703035">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;The&nbsp;language&nbsp;around&nbsp;&quot;events&quot;&nbsp;is&nbsp;stilted</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;&nbsp;The&nbsp;language&nbsp;around&nbsp;&quot;events&quot;&nbsp;is&nbsp;stilted</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I can't seem to get that right. I first had 'anomalous events', then 'SCP-XXXX events', then 'Events' as a predefined terminology, then just 'events', and every time someone complains that something sounds off. I'm not saying the complaints are invalid, just that I have not yet been able to fix this particular issue.</p>
<p>I suspect that this arises due to the simple number of times the term - whatever it may be - has to be used. Eventually any word, when said enough, starts to sound weird. I could be wrong about that being the cause though; do you have any suggestions about how to fix it?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;you&nbsp;repeat&nbsp;some&nbsp;things&nbsp;between&nbsp;the&nbsp;SCP&nbsp;and&nbsp;description</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;&nbsp;you&nbsp;repeat&nbsp;some&nbsp;things&nbsp;between&nbsp;the&nbsp;SCP&nbsp;and&nbsp;description</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>If you are referring to the repeating of the 'events' definition (&quot;henceforth&#8230;&quot;), someone else had the same complaint; if I define the term in the containment procedures, is that fine for the whole document? I figured that there may be many who start with the description, so I had it in both&#8230;</p>
<p>Also, to what else do you refer?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;I&nbsp;also&nbsp;feel&nbsp;like&nbsp;there's&nbsp;too&nbsp;much&nbsp;detail&nbsp;given.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;&nbsp;I&nbsp;also&nbsp;feel&nbsp;like&nbsp;there's&nbsp;too&nbsp;much&nbsp;detail&nbsp;given.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The foundation knows almost exactly how this thing works. It may be weird, but it's completely understood.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's actually part of what was intended to make this SCP unique; they have a <em>really</em> good grasp on what it does and the rules it follows, but by its nature it is still extremely unpredictable and thus can cause unforeseen incidents. Its a juxtaposition.</p>
<p>In addition to the (in my opinion) quaint symmetry described above, the predictability that the lamp does possess is also intended as a 'balancing' mechanic. For example:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'd like it better if they didn't have an equation predicting precisely when it's going to jump.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If that was the case you would have an object that is completely un-containable (it will, without fail, break out in a matter of weeks every time), needs constant maintenance to even track (the live mouse must be maintained at all times), could disrupt any ongoing procedure globally at any time, and could at best cause a convicted death row inmate (D-Class used to maintain the mouse) to die a horrible painful death (if present for the now-unforeseeable events) or at worst release said convicted death row inmate into an urban area.</p>
<p>I actually like that it has some form of a 'weakness', so to speak, in that they know exactly <em>when</em> it will jump. They just have positively no clue <em>where</em>. That one variable is enough to make the lamp quite formidable on its own, as many of the Events demonstrated. Also, remember that another Event 2609-64 might happen, where they don't get to it in time to determine when the next jump will occur before said 'next jump' happens.</p>
<p>This could just be an opinion thing, though. Tell me what you think!</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;I&nbsp;recommend&nbsp;deleting&nbsp;words.&nbsp;Lots&nbsp;of&nbsp;words.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;&nbsp;I&nbsp;recommend&nbsp;deleting&nbsp;words.&nbsp;Lots&nbsp;of&nbsp;words.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>You're not the first to say this, and its definitely something I need to work on. Thank you!</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Thank you for you time and feedback!</p>

							</div>

								
					
