<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Must&nbsp;write&nbsp;shorter&nbsp;posts</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;I&nbsp;keep&nbsp;having&nbsp;to&nbsp;spend&nbsp;ten&nbsp;minutes&nbsp;finding&nbsp;the&nbsp;code&nbsp;for&nbsp;these&nbsp;things</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>1. Basically, I don't feel the discussion on whether to destroy it or not adds anything to the article.</p>
<p>2. Part of it, as Khaim says, is fatigue. The rest of it is that it doesn't really go anywhere that isn't the sort of thing you could guess from the description and effects. Put another way, it's the sort of stuff that, from an in-universe perspective, it makes sense to include, but as someone reading a work of fiction, it doesn't greatly keep me invested. Rereading it though, I do like the plane one&#8230;</p>
<p>3. Yeah, this is more the latter, because, at least in this case, 2609 causes the incident, but it doesn't really <em>need</em> to be 2609, it could have been the doctors being distracted by a fire alarm or something. Basically, it's the sort of thing that would make more sense on 523's page, because 523's the main star, and I can't help but feel like that would be the case for most incidents. I'd be interested to see any other possible ideas you had, though.</p>
<p>4. In all honesty, quite possibly. This goes back to what I was saying earlier; if you're going for a flat-out document style, this makes sense, don't get me wrong, I just prefer some sort of progression in the more clinical skips to keep me engaged, or something short and snappy. The order swapping isn't a bad idea, I guess. It'd probably keep me up when I started flagging.</p>
<p>5. No worries, it's the sort of thing that's easily rectified.</p>
<p>6. The main ones I was thinking of were the rock-eggs (otherwise they just felt a bit thrown in) or one or two of the potential applications. In hindsight, I'd be curious to know where the displaced matter goes, but that's not super-important.</p>
</div>
